WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.840
Film Courage: Can you tell us a time in your
life when you were the most broke making films?

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.120
Dale Stelly, Filmmaker: Most broke?

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.700
Man…I remember…true story.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:21.370
2006 I moved my office to Beverly Hills, I
had an office off of Beverly Drive.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:28.140
It was in a famous building, ICM was in there.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.140
I was in Spike Lee’s old office.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:40.860
I remember I was still getting his mail and
for his manager or producing partner or something.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:47.690
And I had started my business out of my apartment
and sold a couple of films and I’m like

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:52.600
alright, I’m going to take my business and
I’m trying to grow.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.320
So I had agreed to open up this office in
Beverly Hills.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.430
I remember the rent was high as hell.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:04.409
So I had my rent for the office and I had
my rent for where I was living and I ended

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:10.679
up partnering with another friend of mine
at the back lot of Universal and that was

00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:12.789
some money involved there, too.

00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:22.439
I remember in 2006 I shot three movies and
it wasn’t easy and I remember after shooting

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:28.270
those three movies it had just exhausted all
of my funds.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.740
I remember one of those movies kind of went
over budget or something like that.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.579
So it was like ah…man!

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:48.710
I got hit with not having money to pay my
rent for the office…or I had to get out

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:49.710
of there.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.530
One of those things, I couldn’t afford to
stay there.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:02:00.459
So I wound up moving out of the office to
save some money there and I had these three

00:02:00.459 --> 00:02:08.500
films SINGLE BLACK FEMALE, UNEMPLOYED, and
this movie WHAT UP?

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:14.790
And I don’t know how I managed to shoot
those three movies in one year but it tapped

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.180
out my funds and I had to sell the movies
to get everything back and going.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:27.019
But selling three movies is not easy because
they were different movies and I remember

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:37.540
people offering me not the right offers for
my films and I remember that I had to figure

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.209
some stuff out.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:49.299
I ended up luckily at the end of the year
after basically being at the end of my savings

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:50.349
and everything.

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:52.849
I remember my [car] tags were expired.

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:58.610
I couldn’t afford to register my car because
I was just holding onto these movies and I

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:04.049
wouldn’t let them go unless it was like
the right price and I remember staying broke

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:07.379
until the right situation came by.

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:08.690
And I prayed and prayed and prayed.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:14.299
And there were people who were in the position
to help me with distribution and the were

00:03:14.299 --> 00:03:15.439
basically trying to extort me.

00:03:15.439 --> 00:03:17.379
They were trying to get my movies for little
to nothing.

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:22.840
They thought I was desperate because some
close friends of mine kind of knew my personal

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.950
situation that were in the business.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.239
They were trying to take my movie from me
for little or nothing.

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:34.000
And I refused because I know my value and
I know the value of the current marketplace

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.859
and I’m like “I’m going to hold out.”

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:43.920
By the grace of God I was able to sell two
of my movies to Lionsgate.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.420
One of them was UNEMPLOYED and the other one
was through my other partner, called WHAT

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.420
UP?

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.999
They both went through Lionsgate.

00:03:50.999 --> 00:03:56.010
And I brokered one of the deals on my own
so I didn’t have to owe anyone a finder’s

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:00.150
fee or a producer rep fee or anything like
that, you know what I mean?

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:05.670
But I held out and I went from nothing to
something real quick.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:14.959
But I remember that year going into 2008 was
pretty rough but it started in 2007.

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:17.549
Around 2007 I had to make a fight, a comeback.

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:28.240
And I didn’t make a comeback until I sold
my films to Lionsgate.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:36.360
But there was a period where all the money
I had was going to rent just to keep my operation

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.360
going.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.590
Just to keep the doors open in Beverly Hills.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:45.930
It was like Oh man…you know my ego and everything
was coming in play…and it was like “Oh

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:46.930
man!”

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:52.070
And I was about to move my office back home
or whatever the case may be but as technology

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:58.160
and everything changed and things became more
expensive people like myself started to have

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.219
a home office anyway because of Internet and
everything, it’s easier.

00:05:02.219 --> 00:05:08.470
So now I’ll work in an office when I’m
in production, so I’ll rent the office space

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:12.020
when I’m in production for three or four
months for the duration of production and

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:17.410
then take everything else back to home base
because it’s just more economical for me

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.580
that way because everything is expensive in
LA.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:25.400
Like rent alone can run you 2,000 for the
right place, you know what I mean?

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.440
You’re trying to have that professional
facade, you’re trying to charge certain

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.450
rates and everything so it costs to be the
boss.

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:40.479
Film Courage: That’s interesting because
I think this is a story that a lot of people

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:46.449
in LA had kind of had something nice and then
it took a dip and they’ve come back (at

00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:47.960
least the ones who have stayed).

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:52.860
So once you come back what’s your mindset
knowing that (because you said you went from

00:05:52.860 --> 00:06:00.039
nothing to something) but I’m sure those
feelings of having to move out of that office

00:06:00.039 --> 00:06:01.240
don’t go away.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.050
I mean you still remember that.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:08.410
Dale: Yes I do and to be honest I wish I’d
never moved into that office because I would

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:14.889
have saved over 30,000 if I’d just kept
to my template of how I was doing things I

00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:22.880
would have been in a better financial situation
but I don’t regret it because everything

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:24.169
in life you learn from.

00:06:24.169 --> 00:06:29.120
You take the good and the bad and you muster
it up into a lesson.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.270
So I was very glad to say “Hey I had an
office.”

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:38.750
Spike Lee’s old office, I was working with
a big heavyweight producer and right above

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:40.310
Madeo restaurant in Beverly Hills.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:45.360
It was a good experience and I actually produced
movies out of my office.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.919
Some people have offices and then don’t
to any stuff with it, it’s just a front

00:06:48.919 --> 00:06:50.090
of whatever.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:57.970
But I actually had auditions, I had actors
coming in, I actually made the office very

00:06:57.970 --> 00:06:59.449
active and friendly atmosphere.

00:06:59.449 --> 00:07:05.470
The other producers that I was sharing an
office space with they weren’t really as

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:06.530
active as me.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:12.939
Like I have people meeting, coming in, I’m
spending 1,600-1,800 a month on office space.

00:07:12.939 --> 00:07:18.020
I want to make sure I’m doing what I’m
supposed to be doing.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:26.970
And all that energy and meetings and bringing
people to me and trying to manifest productions

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:32.530
and budget it all, I’ve learned from it
and it’s made me who I am today.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:34.379
I don’t regret it, you know what I mean.

00:07:34.379 --> 00:07:39.000
You live and learn and now I’m on a positive
waive right now.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.930
I’ve got a couple of films released into
the marketplace and I’m building off of

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.310
that and trying to slide into TV.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:51.199
It’s just like staying persistent, persistency
is the key… persistency.

